Chapter: Console

 We say:

Section: Definitions

  effect Console where
    print-line-uni : Text -> [Console.Write] Nothing
    read-line  : [Console.Read] Text

Page 1

Chapter: Scenery

 The vocabulary the roadside populations share: what a tree is, what a critter
 is, which colour scheme a segment runs, which big animal stands at its exit --
 and the two distances everything beside the road is measured from.

 Split out of World on 2026-09-03. It exists because `Trees`, `Herd` and `Pigs`
 each build their own population and all three need the same words for it: a
 pig and a cow are both a `Critter`, and both stand `herd-road-offset` off a
 road `lane-width` wide. Nothing here generates anything.

 We say:

Section: The Road

  lane-width : Real
  lane-width = 4.0

 Longer segments stand their own tower halfway down.

  herd-road-offset : Real
  herd-road-offset = 10.0

Section: Schemes and Creatures

  Scheme =
   | AllGreen
   | YellowGreen
   | RedGreen

 The big animal parked at a segment's EXIT corner. Elephant is the unmarked
 default; the rest are the rare departures. DuckPond is a hand-drawn scene rather
 than an emoji pair, and NoCreature is the terminus, which has no exit corner.

 The constructor is DuckPond rather than Pond because chapters share one flat
 namespace inside a bundle (CDX3001) and `Safari chapter Pond` is already in this
 port -- a collision that would only appear once something cited both.


  Creature =
   | NoCreature
   | Elephant
   | Giraffe
   | Zebra
   | Rhino
   | DuckPond

Section: What Stands Beside the Road

  Tree = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    color : Integer,
    height : Real
  }

 A roadside animal: an emoji billboard at along and across-from-centre.


  Critter = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    codepoint : Integer,
    height : Real,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

 A pond is a Creature that is not an animal, which the ground walk asks about to
 place water and the collection asks about to place ducks.

  is-pond : Creature -> Boolean
  is-pond (c) =
   when c
    is DuckPond -> True
    is NoCreature -> False
    is Elephant -> False
    is Giraffe -> False
    is Zebra -> False
    is Rhino -> False

Chapter: Trees
  cites Safari chapter Scenery

 The conifer rows: their colours, their spacing, and the pair-at-a-time fill
 that lays them down the length of a segment.

 Split out of World on 2026-09-03, and the split moved the palette with it. The
 three conifer colours sat under a heading called `Palette and Dimensions` and
 were reachable only through `fill-trees` -- so they were never a palette, they
 were the tree colours, and they belong here.

 We say:

Section: Colours

  conifer-green : Integer
  conifer-green = 1858082

  conifer-gold : Integer
  conifer-gold = 13605400

  conifer-red : Integer
  conifer-red = 11680298

Section: Dimensions

  small-height : Real
  small-height = 4.5

  big-scale : Real
  big-scale = 1.3

 Metres between trees, and this is FIXED where the TypeScript fixes the COUNT --
 so density, and the speed illusion it creates, is uniform across segments.


  tree-spacing : Real
  tree-spacing = 30.0

  tree-road-offset : Real
  tree-road-offset = 1.5

  tree-start-inset : Real
  tree-start-inset = 6.0

  tree-end-inset : Real
  tree-end-inset = 85.0

  max-trees : Integer
  max-trees = 96

Section: Filling a Segment

  accent-color : Scheme -> Integer
  accent-color (s) =
   when s
    is YellowGreen -> conifer-gold
    is RedGreen -> conifer-red
    is AllGreen -> conifer-green

 Conifers every tree-spacing metres from the start inset to the end-clear zone.
 Even index is green and 1.3 times tall, odd is the scheme's accent. Red trees are
 twice everything; gold three times and set about four times further off the road.

 The pair is emitted together, so the cap is checked two at a time exactly as the
 zig's `n_trees + 2 <= MAX_TREES` does.


  tree-height-for : Integer, Boolean -> Real
  tree-height-for (color) (even) =
    let base = if even then small-height * big-scale else small-height
    in if color == conifer-red then base * 2.0 else (if color == conifer-gold then base * 3.0 else base)

  tree-x-for : Integer -> Real
  tree-x-for (color) =
    if color == conifer-gold then lane-width / 2.0 + 4.0 * tree-road-offset
    else lane-width / 2.0 + tree-road-offset

  fill-trees : Scheme, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List Tree
  fill-trees (scheme) (length) (along) (k) (n) =
    if along > length - tree-end-inset then []
    else if n + 2 > max-trees then []
    else fill-trees-pair scheme length along k n

  fill-trees-pair : Scheme, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List Tree
  fill-trees-pair (scheme) (length) (along) (k) (n) =
    let even = k - ((k / 2) * 2) == 0
    in let color = if even then conifer-green else accent-color scheme
    in let height = tree-height-for color even
    in let x = tree-x-for color
    in [Tree { along = along, across = 0.0 - x, color = color, height = height }, Tree { along = along, across = x, color = color, height = height }] & fill-trees scheme length (along + tree-spacing) (k + 1) (n + 2)

Chapter: Grade

 The reusable grader: compare a flattened list of values against gold and say
 WHERE the first disagreement is.

 Every seam in this port reduces to the same shape -- a list of Reals, a list of
 Integers, a list of Booleans -- so one grader serves pond, the camera, and later
 the draw-command buffer, which is itself a flat list. Flattening at the seam is
 what makes that true.

 The judge is built out of nothing it judges: g-abs duplicates real-abs on
 purpose. DeviceMath's own accuracy test records why -- when its judge called the
 chapter under test, collapsing that chapter left every cell reading ok.

 A failure names the INDEX, not just BAD. `show` on a Real is refused by the zig
 plug, which needs a __real_to_text it does not have, so a Real cannot be printed
 to say what it was; an Integer index can, and it points at one value instead of
 at a whole seam. When real-to-int lands, this is the chapter that grows a
 scaled-integer dump and the tolerance argument goes away.

 We say:

Section: Judging Reals

  g-abs : Real -> Real
  g-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

 A NaN USED TO PASS EVERY GATE IN THIS CHAPTER, silently, and it is worth saying
 exactly how. Each grader below asks "is |got - want| GREATER THAN the tolerance".
 Subtract a NaN and the difference is a NaN; `g-abs` of a NaN is a NaN, because
 `NaN < 0.0` is false and the else arm hands it back; and `NaN > tol` is false, so
 the value is not flagged and the walk moves on. Infinity was always caught --
 `inf - want` is `inf`, which does exceed any tolerance -- but a NaN is invisible
 to a comparison-based gate BY CONSTRUCTION, and there were tens of thousands of
 Reals a run passing through gates shaped exactly like this.

 IT WAS ALSO UNDETECTABLE UNTIL 2026-08-30. `show` on a Real is refused by the zig
 plug, and a NaN is the one value that does not equal itself, so no comparison and
 no printing available to this chapter could see one. `real-to-bits` is what
 changed: it landed in the zig plug that day (plugs-backlog 2.07) and a bit pattern
 does not care whether the value has an equal.

 THE TEST IS ONE MASK AND ONE COMPARISON. Clear the sign bit and every finite
 double is below #7FF0000000000000; positive infinity IS that number and every NaN
 is above it. So one gate catches both, and it is exact rather than approximate.

  g-finite : Real -> Boolean
  g-finite (x) = bit-and (real-to-bits x) #7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF < #7FF0000000000000

  first-real-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-real-diff (got) (want) (tol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if g-finite (list-at got i) then
      (if g-abs (list-at got i - list-at want i) > tol then i
       else first-real-diff got want tol (i + 1))
    else i

  grade-reals : Text, List Real, List Real, Real -> Text
  grade-reals (name) (got) (want) (tol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-real-diff got want tol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Section: Judging Reals Relatively

 A RELATIVE tolerance, scaled by the larger of 1 and the wanted magnitude -- the
 form DeviceMath's own accuracy test uses. Computed values need it: the port
 works in Real (f64) while the game computes in f32, so a projected pixel and a
 focal length disagree in proportion to their size, not by a fixed amount. An
 absolute tolerance that passes a 0.9 duck height would wave through a 4-pixel
 error on a 685-pixel focal.

  g-max : Real, Real -> Real
  g-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  first-rel-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-rel-diff (got) (want) (tol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if g-finite (list-at got i) then
      (let w = list-at want i
       in if g-abs (list-at got i - w) > tol * g-max 1.0 (g-abs w) then i
       else first-rel-diff got want tol (i + 1))
    else i

  grade-rel : Text, List Real, List Real, Real -> Text
  grade-rel (name) (got) (want) (tol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-rel-diff got want tol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Section: Judging Cancelled Coordinates

 A MIXED tolerance: an absolute floor plus a relative term, which is the only form
 that fits a value produced by cancellation. Neither half works alone, and both
 failures were measured rather than guessed.

 IT IS NAMED FOR THE FAILURE MODE, NOT THE UNIT, and `grade-px` keeps its name
 because screen pixels were the first seam to need it and most of its callers are
 still projections. RenderCheck grades rider-frame METRES with it: `at` composes a
 point down a kilometre of chain and returns one a metre or two off the rider's
 nose, which is the same small-difference-of-two-large-numbers this gate exists
 for, in a different unit. The FLOOR is what changes with the unit -- a thousandth
 of a pixel on a canvas, an f32 ulp at the range a chain reaches -- and D5's rule
 is that it is calibrated per seam and never inherited.

 A RELATIVE gate fails near the horizon. A screen y is 300 minus a term of about
 the same size, so a y of 20.6 is a small difference of two large numbers and
 cancellation amplifies f32's relative error by the ratio between them -- fifteen
 times epsilon on the first cone tier of a near tree.

 An ABSOLUTE gate fails far off screen. Projecting a point 37 m to the side at the
 0.4 m near plane puts x at about -62929, where one f32 ulp is already 0.0075 px --
 seven times a thousandth-of-a-pixel floor, before any port is involved.

 The floor governs coordinates near zero and on the canvas; the relative term
 governs the far-off-screen ones the near plane throws out. At a hundred pixels the
 gate is about a thousandth of a pixel, which is the precision worth asserting.

  grade-px : Text, List Real, List Real, Real, Real -> Text
  grade-px (name) (got) (want) (atol) (rtol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-px-diff got want atol rtol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

  first-px-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-px-diff (got) (want) (atol) (rtol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if g-finite (list-at got i) then
      (let w = list-at want i
       in if g-abs (list-at got i - w) > atol + rtol * g-abs w then i
       else first-px-diff got want atol rtol (i + 1))
    else i

Section: Judging Integers

  first-int-diff : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  first-int-diff (got) (want) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if list-at got i /= list-at want i then i
    else first-int-diff got want (i + 1)

  grade-ints : Text, List Integer, List Integer -> Text
  grade-ints (name) (got) (want) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-int-diff got want 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i & " got " & show (list-at got i) & " want " & show (list-at want i)

Section: Judging Booleans

  bool-eq : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  bool-eq (a) (b) = if a then b else if b then False else True

  first-bool-diff : List Boolean, List Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  first-bool-diff (got) (want) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if bool-eq (list-at got i) (list-at want i) then first-bool-diff got want (i + 1)
    else i

  grade-bools : Text, List Boolean, List Boolean -> Text
  grade-bools (name) (got) (want) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-bool-diff got want 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Chapter: TreesSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Trees
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Trees: the pair-at-a-time fill, and the two things that stop it.

 Nothing in judge/ grades this chapter. It reaches the frame only through World,
 which builds every segment's trees at startup, so a fill that laid down one
 pair too many or stopped a pair too early would arrive as a whole-frame diff.

 THE FILL EMITS A PAIR AT A TIME AND THE CAP IS CHECKED TWO AT A TIME, which is
 the zig's `n_trees + 2 <= MAX_TREES` and is why the cap can only ever be hit
 exactly. A 5,000 m segment is graded below for precisely 96 trees -- not 95,
 not 97 -- and a fill that checked one at a time would give 97 on some segment
 and nothing would say so.

 THE END INSET IS A BOUNDARY AND IT IS GRADED AS ONE. The walk stops when
 `along > length - tree-end-inset`, so on a 300 m segment it stops above 215 and
 the last pair is laid at 186. A pair at 216 would be the off-by-one, and the
 last `along` below is what refuses it.

 WHERE THE EXPECTED VALUES COME FROM: the rules in the chapter's own prose --
 every 30 m from the 6 m inset, even index green and 1.3 times tall, odd the
 scheme's accent, red twice everything, gold three times and about four times
 further off the road -- worked out in doubles. The heights are the one place
 that is not tidy: 4.5 * 1.3 is 5.8500000000000005 and the want says so rather
 than rounding it and reaching for a tolerance.

 We say:

Section: The Palette And The Dimensions

  colour-got : List Integer
  colour-got = [conifer-green, conifer-gold, conifer-red, max-trees]

  colour-want : List Integer
  colour-want = [1858082, 13605400, 11680298, 96]

  dim-got : List Real
  dim-got = [small-height, big-scale, tree-spacing, tree-road-offset, tree-start-inset, tree-end-inset]

  dim-want : List Real
  dim-want = [4.5, 1.3, 30.0, 1.5, 6.0, 85.0]

Section: The Accent, All Three Schemes

 Five of six arms of the two lookups answer the same colour as some other arm,
 so all three schemes are graded rather than the interesting one.

  accent-got : List Integer
  accent-got = [accent-color AllGreen, accent-color YellowGreen, accent-color RedGreen]

  accent-want : List Integer
  accent-want = [1858082, 13605400, 11680298]

 Height and offset for each colour in both parities. An even tree is 1.3 times
 the small height whatever its colour; red doubles and gold triples on top of
 that, and only gold moves off the road.

  hx-got : List Real
  hx-got =
    [ tree-height-for 1858082 True, tree-height-for 1858082 False,
      tree-height-for 11680298 True, tree-height-for 11680298 False,
      tree-height-for 13605400 True, tree-height-for 13605400 False,
      tree-x-for 1858082, tree-x-for 11680298, tree-x-for 13605400 ]

  hx-want : List Real
  hx-want = [5.8500000000000005, 4.5, 11.700000000000001, 9.0, 17.55, 13.5, 3.5, 3.5, 8.0]

Section: A Filled Segment

 Three hundred metres of YellowGreen, which alternates green pairs at 3.5 m with
 gold pairs out at 8.0 m -- the scheme that exercises both offsets.

  filled : List Tree
  filled = fill-trees YellowGreen 300.0 tree-start-inset 0 0

  along-got : List Real
  along-got = list-map (\t -> t.along) filled

  along-want : List Real
  along-want = [6.0, 6.0, 36.0, 36.0, 66.0, 66.0, 96.0, 96.0, 126.0, 126.0, 156.0, 156.0, 186.0, 186.0]

 Each pair straddles the road, so the across values come in mirrored twos.

  across-got : List Real
  across-got = list-map (\t -> t.across) filled

  across-want : List Real
  across-want = [-3.5, 3.5, -8.0, 8.0, -3.5, 3.5, -8.0, 8.0, -3.5, 3.5, -8.0, 8.0, -3.5, 3.5]

  colours-got : List Integer
  colours-got = list-map (\t -> t.color) filled

  colours-want : List Integer
  colours-want = [1858082, 1858082, 13605400, 13605400, 1858082, 1858082, 13605400, 13605400, 1858082, 1858082, 13605400, 13605400, 1858082, 1858082]

  heights-got : List Real
  heights-got = list-map (\t -> t.height) filled

  heights-want : List Real
  heights-want = [5.8500000000000005, 5.8500000000000005, 13.5, 13.5, 5.8500000000000005, 5.8500000000000005, 13.5, 13.5, 5.8500000000000005, 5.8500000000000005, 13.5, 13.5, 5.8500000000000005, 5.8500000000000005]

Section: The Two Stops

 A short segment lays nothing at all -- 100 m less the 85 m end inset leaves 15,
 and the 6 m start inset is inside it, so one pair goes down and the next is
 refused. A 5,000 m segment hits the cap exactly.

  stops-got : List Integer
  stops-got =
    [ list-length filled,
      list-length (fill-trees AllGreen 100.0 tree-start-inset 0 0),
      list-length (fill-trees AllGreen 5000.0 tree-start-inset 0 0),
      list-length (fill-trees AllGreen 50.0 tree-start-inset 0 0) ]

  stops-want : List Integer
  stops-want = [14, 2, 96, 0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "tr-colour" colour-got colour-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-dim   " dim-got dim-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "tr-accent" accent-got accent-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-hx    " hx-got hx-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-along " along-got along-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-across" across-got across-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "tr-colours" colours-got colours-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-height" heights-got heights-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "tr-stops " stops-got stops-want)
  end
